= Agent initialization — Technical =

== Bootstrap as state reconstruction ==
For a persistent agent, initialization can be treated as construction of an execution-ready state <code>X_0</code> from multiple authorities:

<pre>
X_0 = R(I, V, C, P, M, G, E, H)
</pre>

where:

* <code>I</code> = persistent identity;
* <code>V</code> = software/model/configuration version;
* <code>C</code> = constraints/policy;
* <code>P</code> = permissions/delegation;
* <code>M</code> = persistent memory/checkpoint state;
* <code>G</code> = goals/commitments;
* <code>E</code> = environment/tool context;
* <code>H</code> = provenance/history.

<code>R</code> is not necessarily a pure function: reconciliation may reject inconsistent inputs or require external validation.

== Suggested dependency order ==
A defensible persistent-agent bootstrap is:

<pre>
1. resolve persistent identity
2. load versioned configuration/policy
3. establish authentication and current authority
4. discover/validate environment and tools
5. retrieve identity- and scope-bound state
6. validate provenance, freshness, and consistency
7. restore goals/commitments
8. register run/provenance state
9. enter normal reasoning loop
</pre>

This ordering is an engineering synthesis rather than a universal protocol.

== Fresh start versus continuation ==
A runtime should distinguish operations explicitly:

<pre>
create(I_new)
restart(I_existing)
resume(I_existing, checkpoint)
recover(I_existing, evidence)
fork(I_parent → I_child)
</pre>

A fork that silently reuses the parent's identity creates ambiguity over future state ownership.

== BDI correspondence ==
Classical BDI implementations commonly initialize a belief base, goals, plan library, and event/intention state before entering the deliberation cycle.

Persistent foundation-model agents generalize the same idea but often externalize significant state into databases, retrieval systems, credentials, and tool registries.

== Validation conditions ==
Useful initialization checks include:

* identifier/namespace consistency;
* schema/version compatibility;
* permission freshness;
* checkpoint integrity;
* provenance availability;
* absence of contradictory authoritative records;
* state ownership/scope match;
* explicit handling of missing state.

Fail-open reconstruction can convert missing evidence into implicit assumptions.

== Initialization failure amplification ==
If initialization chooses the wrong <code>X_0</code>, subsequent reasoning can be locally correct relative to the wrong starting state.

This is why initialization errors can be harder to detect than isolated action errors.

